Git Notes
git add · git commit · git diff · git diff --staged · git mv · git restore · git merge

1. The Big Picture — Git's Three Areas
Every command below moves a file between three areas, or lets you compare them.
  WORKING DIR  --git add-->  STAGING AREA  --git commit-->  REPOSITORY
  (edited files)              (index)                     (.git history)

       <----------- git restore -----------|
                     <---- git restore --staged ----|


2. git add   [Working -> Staging]
Moves changes from the working directory into the staging area, marking them 'ready to commit.'
git add filename.txt      # stage one file
git add .                 # stage everything in current folder
git add -A                # stage all changes in repo
Practice:  Edit a file, run 'git status' (shows red/untracked), then 'git add' it and run 'git status' again (shows green/staged).

3. git commit   [Staging -> Repository]
Saves the staged snapshot permanently into the project's history with a message.
git commit -m "Add login page"
git commit -am "Fix typo"   # add + commit tracked files in one step
  (commit 1) ---> (commit 2) ---> (commit 3 - HEAD)
Practice:  Stage 2-3 files separately, commit them, then run 'git log --oneline' to see the history chain.

4. git diff   [Working vs Staging]
Shows line-by-line changes that are NOT yet staged — i.e. working directory vs staging area.
git diff              # unstaged changes

git diff filename.txt # for one file
  WORKING DIR  <== git diff shows THIS gap ==>  STAGING AREA  ---->  REPOSITORY
Practice:  Edit a tracked file (don't stage it) then run 'git diff' — observe red (-) old lines and green (+) new lines.

5. git diff --staged   [Staging vs Repository]
Shows what will actually go into the next commit — staging area vs last commit. (git diff --cached is the same thing, older name.)
git diff --staged
git diff --cached     # same thing, older name
  WORKING DIR  ---->  STAGING AREA  <== git diff --staged shows THIS gap ==>  REPO / HEAD

Command	Compares
git diff	Working Directory <-> Staging Area
git diff --staged	Staging Area <-> Last Commit
Practice:  Stage a change, then run both 'git diff' (empty!) and 'git diff --staged' (shows the change) to feel the difference.

6. git mv   [Rename / Move]
Renames or moves a file and stages that change in one step (equivalent to a manual rename plus 'git add' on both the old and new name).
git mv old_name.txt new_name.txt
git mv file.txt folder/file.txt
  old_name.txt   --git mv-->   new_name.txt   (already staged)
Practice:  Rename a file using 'git mv', then run 'git status' — it should show as 'renamed', already staged.

7. git restore   [Undo Changes]
Restores files — discards working directory edits, or unstages a file.
git restore filename.txt            # discard unstaged edits (working dir)
git restore --staged filename.txt   # unstage (move staged -> working dir, keep edits)
  REPOSITORY   --git restore file (reverts to last commit)-->  WORKING DIR
  STAGING AREA --git restore --staged file----------------->  WORKING DIR
Practice:  Stage a change, then 'git restore --staged' it (still edited, just unstaged). Then 'git restore' it (edit fully discarded).

8. git merge   [Combine Branches]
Combines the history of another branch into your current branch.
git checkout main
git merge feature-branch
  main:      C1 -------------------- C2 --- Merge
                \                          /
  feature:        F1 --- F2 ---------------
Practice:  Create a branch, add a commit on it, switch back to main, add another commit, then merge the branch in and observe the merge commit.

9. Quick Recap Table
Command	Purpose
git add	Working -> Staging
git commit	Staging -> Repository
git diff	Working vs Staging
git diff --staged	Staging vs Repository
git mv	Rename/move + auto-stage
git restore	Undo changes / unstage
